commentBody:
@Barry Ancona

I’m surprised the you wouldn’t rank highly a puzzle with CANTANKEROUS in it 😀

the revealer came late for me so I found it appropriately tough. There were trivia re blue mana, order of the Old Testament, a newspaper, football, and the crossing of themed entries that created enough of a bulwark for a Thursday.

@SP

Thank you for the explanation. That's the sort of thing that will probably always remain arcane for a non-native English speaker living in a non-English-speaking country. 

I'm slowly tuning into your style of clueing, btw, and I'm beginning to enjoy your puzzles more than most that get published by the NYT. Also, I like how that platform doesn't track your streak (maybe it does if you log in? But I'm obsessive about not being tracked online so I never log in into anything with Google).

@Dale M

No, SBK is the person who eavesdrops on other people's conversation and complains they don't get it all.

(This is not the first time that SBK complains about posts that had nothing to do with her)

@Red Carpet

Nicely put. 

When I start solving a puzzle like this, where answers just don’t make sense given the clue, I take a deep breath and remind myself that eventually, I’ll understand what is going on. And 99.9% of the time, I do.

@Red Carpet
Fair Thursday is overly generous. This was Tuesday - Wednesday level. The trick wasn't even that hard to suss out.

And the revealer makes no sense at all. They could have clued FIRE BLANKS in so many better ways. It may be a very oblique way of indicating that at the start of a footrace, they fire a starter's pistol, but this is a very loose association. On the whole, Thursdays no longer hold the joy they once did. Twas my favorite dya of the week, but alas, twas not meant to be.

@acjones

Most of us assume that the column and the comments will contain spoilers. Anyone new to Wordplay quickly learns that is so.

It’s virtually impossible to say anything meaningful about a puzzle if you try to avoid spoilers.

@dutchiris
May I add some emphasis to something you mentioned en passant: a grid free of obscure proper names! This is such an irritant to so 
many and Ms/Mr Fritz avoided it. Bravo, McKay.

@acjones

you wanted HURTLES, not HURDLES....
But I appreciate your pointing out that the FIREball is not a mistake...

@acjones

Visible fire is glowing soot from the fuel heated like an electrical filament. A comet's tail* is electrically charged gas molecules (ions) ionized by ultraviolet light from the sun. It's incandescence vs ionization - not the same. 

*It has two tails but we won't get into that.

@Andre

Oh c’mon! You knew it was only four letters! That narrows it down a lot. 

Still took me getting the B from the fairly predictable IBM to come up with the right city.

@Miranda

I enjoy hearing and playing MLR too but as far as I’m aware it doesn’t have words and as such isn’t a song.  Scott Joplin did write one opera called Treemonisha but his famous ragtime piano compositions are not songs 🤪

Mark

@Miranda
Joplin's rags are great stuff. Alas, I am of a generation for wgich they are indelibly associated with that cynical manipulative long-con of a filn, The Stink.
Sorry, The Sting.

@Andrzej

I read your linked article with only mild interest until the chart that showed shop-bought beer prices had increased 49%.  

I feel your pain.

@Jeb
It's correct, and unambiguous.
The key of A-flat Major has 4 flats.

@D 

Sometimes you defeat them. Sometimes they defeat you.

Don't take it personally. They're supposed to be challenging.

@Jim I think that's often right, but sometimes the vitriol comes from people who describe themselves as veterans, and even from people who have a considerably long streak. 

Well, I understand that breaking a streak is frustrating (I avoid the frustration by not paying attention - I'm sure I would never get to triple digits anyway). 

But the sense of anger on display, sometimes, is confusing. To be sure, it's probably rarely as seriously felt as it might appear in writing, but even so - sometimes the comments seem quite hurtful, and based on an expectation that the puzzle should always be the right level of challenging and solvable *to that particular solver*, which is an impossible standard.

@Ben Powers

Yes, although the fill was pretty evident, a COMET is anything but a fireball.  Closer to a dirty snowball, as astronomers describe them.  

The theme was pretty evident, especially after finding the revealer.

@Lee
Aye, you are one of the hundred corrections I'm expecting.  Them's the breaks.  You go along for decades enjoying "the Comet that the Solon Comets saw" atory, and then some twelve dozen folks pop the balloon.  wah wah waaaah (trombone sound)
But thanks for joining the party!

@valb

You're right and the puzzle is right. I'm not a coder but someone explained it earlier in the comments.

But I think the OP meant that the "!" he used wasn’t a negation of "Fun puzzle."

@John
If you see one from the ground, it sorta does!  During a HS football game, DHubby and the kids got to see a FIREball shoot over the field--later ascertained to have been, in fact, a COMET.
Fun fact:  the football team was (is) The Solon COMETS!

@Jeb and @ Charles
Yeah, after I read enough Comments, I was expecting your call.  
Of course, The Comets on the  playing field were happiest to call it that instead of a meteor, but Oh, Well.....

@AG

The clue is “fireball” which solves to “comet.”  (Though, comets are iceballs, but…) 

“Comet ball” is an incorrect reading of the theme.

@Dale M
Cool has always been hot. Likewise, hot has always been cool.
Get used to it, babe. 😳

@Peter C.

"if a clue leads you to the correct answer,"

Well said!

@Lynn
I think this is under Rule 2:
'Yes, but check anyway.'
